  <!-- 1080p vs 4K -->
  <h2 id="1080p-vs-4k">Does 4K Actually Matter for Calls?</h2>
  <p>
    For most video calls? Nope.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zoom caps at 1080p even if you pay them. Free accounts get 720p. Google Meet does about the same—1080p max but
    usually less. Teams? Same ceiling. And most calls auto-adjust quality based on your connection anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    4K matters when you're recording for YouTube. Or cropping and reframing in post. Photography stuff. Future-proofing
    maybe.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bottom line:</strong> A solid 1080p webcam like the C920s works perfectly fine for most people. Only go 4K
    if you're actually creating video content.
  </p>

  <!-- Built-in vs External -->
  <h2 id="builtin-vs-external">External vs Built-in: Should You Upgrade?</h2>
  <p>
    Laptop webcams have gotten better. Dedicated ones still win though.
  </p>

  <h3>Time to upgrade when:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your laptop has 720p (many still do in 2025—insane)</li>
    <li>You're doing video calls every day</li>
    <li>Your angle makes people look up your nose</li>
    <li>Someone's complained about your quality</li>
    <li>Your lighting is bad</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Built-in might work if:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You've got a recent MacBook with M-series—those cameras are actually pretty good now</li>
    <li>Calls are occasional</li>
    <li>Portability matters more</li>
    <li>Your laptop is already at eye level</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Lighting Tips -->
  <h2 id="lighting-tips">Lighting Tips</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the thing: lighting matters more than your webcam. A $300 camera with bad lighting looks worse than a $70 one
    with good lighting. Seen it happen many times.
  </p>

  <h3>Basics:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Face a window when possible—natural light flatters</li>
    <li>Never have bright light behind you or you'll look like witness protection</li>
    <li>Place lights at roughly 45 degrees to your face</li>
    <li>Diffuse harsh light when you can</li>
    <li>Check what's behind you</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cheap stuff that works:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Basic desk lamp, maybe $20-40</li>
    <li>Ring light, $30-80</li>
    <li>Key light if going pro, $50-150</li>
    <li>Or just rearrange to face a window—free and usually best</li>
  </ul>

